[0.052, "o", "\u001b[35m\u001b[3mexec\u001b[0m\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /private/tmp/skills-demo-sams-env/home/.agents/skills/sams-env/SKILL.md; cat /private/tmp/skills-demo-sams-env/home/.agents/skills/sams-env/references/checker.md; cat /private/tmp/skills-demo-sams-env/home/.agents/skills/sams-env/evals/annotated-example.md; cat /private/tmp/skills-demo-sams-env/home/.agents/skills/sams-env/evals/contract.md'\u001b[0m in /private/tmp/skills-demo-sams-env/home/sams-env-demo\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "\u001b[32m succeeded in 0ms:\u001b[0m\r\n---\r\nname: sams-env\r\ndescription: Draft or improve .env.example files from project code and Git-tracked examples, with concise setup links, commands, and value guidance.\r\n---\r\n\r\n# Sam's Env\r\n\r\nWrite a `.env.example` that helps developers configure the project with minimal searching. Keep comments that help them choose, obtain, set, inspect, or troubleshoot a value.\r\n\r\n## Inspect Safely\r\n\r\nRead project instructions, code, configuration schemas, and package scripts. Restrict searches to relevant code and docs; exclude actual env and secret files.\r\n\r\nRead existing env content only from Git-tracked files named exactly `.env.example`. Verify the path with `git ls-files --error-unmatch -- <path>`, then read its committed regular-file blob with `git show HEAD:<path>`. Skip untracked, uncommitted-only, or symlink examples. Never read `.env`, `.env.local`, other actual env files, or their history. Tracking does not guarantee secret-free content; do not reproduce apparent credentials.\r\n\r\nId"]
[0.000, "o", "entify the loader, destination filename, consumed variables, requiredness, defaults, and missing/empty behavior. Check build-time versus runtime use and browser exposure. Flag unknown or stale settings; do not invent them or silently remove supported ones.\r\n\r\n## Write Useful Comments\r\n\r\nGroup related variables. Put core settings first and optional integrations afterward. Give simple settings little or no commentary; expand where a developer would otherwise need to search or guess.\r\n\r\nInclude whichever details help that variable:\r\n\r\n- Exact documentation URLs, preferably the relevant section.\r\n- The exact dashboard page for creating, setting, or inspecting that value. A provider dashboard homepage is not enough. Resolve the value's owner first: an app-owned ADMIN_TOKEN stored as a Worker secret belongs in that Worker's settings, while a Cloudflare API token belongs on the API Tokens page. Use verified deep links or documented account-selector routes without private identifiers. If a precise URL is unavailable,"]
[0.000, "o", " state that and give the exact navigation path; do not guess a route or present a homepage as the answer.\r\n- Verified single-line commands for useful set, get, generate, or check operations. State prerequisites and consequential effects. For secrets, prefer presence checks over commands that print values.\r\n- Non-obvious accepted values or examples, including units, format, and meaningful defaults.\r\n- Required permissions, dependencies, missing/empty behavior, or restart/rebuild rules when they prevent likely mistakes.\r\n\r\nWrite direct instructions with the project's actual names. Delete comments that merely restate the variable name or visible assignment. Put shared guidance once above its group. Keep URLs and command syntax intact; move long procedures and test reports into linked docs. Use no fixed comment quota, universal checklist block, or telegraphic prose.\r\n\r\nUse safe defaults supported by code. Leave secrets blank with acquisition guidance. Comment out optional assignments when an active blank would ov"]
[0.000, "o", "erride the intended default. Label any necessary placeholders unmistakably. Derive permissions and example values from source or provider docs, not guesswork.\r\n\r\nMatch the loader's quoting, comments, and expansion rules. Keep command snippets as comments. Documenting commands does not authorize executing them.\r\n\r\n## Space Variable Blocks\r\n\r\nAdd a blank line before each new explanatory comment block, with its variable directly below. Apply the same rule to commented-out assignments such as `# FEATURE_FLAG=`. Keep consecutive comment lines together; never add blank lines inside an annotation block or between annotations and their variable.\r\n\r\nKeep short related groups compact when they share one explanation. Annotation-only lines do not force a blank between neighboring assignments. Split long optional lists by purpose during drafting; the formatter cannot infer those groups. Put explanations above their variable or group, including fallback-alias notes.\r\n\r\nRun the bundled `scripts/format-env-example.mjs --chec"]
[0.000, "o", "k --stdin` on newly authored text. Use `--format --stdin` to insert missing block spacing. It preserves assignment text, values, order, commented state, and newline style. It refuses ambiguous or multiline syntax. This layout check complements the static annotation checker; neither verifies dashboard destinations or live credentials.\r\n\r\n## Use @env-spec Annotations\r\n\r\nUse standard `# @...` comments for types, requiredness, sensitivity, and documentation. Keep ordinary dotenv assignments so existing loaders can ignore the annotations. Put item annotations directly above their assignment without a blank line. Use `@type=number(isInt=true)` for integers, `@type=port(...)` for ports, and `@type=enum(...)` for choices. String is the default type.\r\n\r\nUse `@docs(URL)` for exact documentation and ordinary comments for dashboard links and one-line commands. Mark optional variables explicitly with `@optional` when appropriate; Varlock treats unmarked items as required by default. Annotations document a contract; they d"]
[0.000, "o", "o not make an ordinary dotenv loader enforce it.\r\n\r\nRead [checker usage and limits](references/checker.md) before using the bundled dependency-free Node.js checker. It supports a static subset and rejects everything outside that subset. Keep cross-variable requirements in prose and application validation unless the checker explicitly supports them. Do not simplify a real requirement just to obtain a passing check.\r\n\r\n## Check and Deliver\r\n\r\nCheck coverage, duplicate assignments, documented commands, and ignore rules without opening local env files. Validate newly authored text in memory with the actual parser; do not reread an untracked output or stage it to satisfy the input restriction. Existing-file validation follows the same tracked-example rule. Use the parser directly; avoid application commands that auto-load local env files. Never source env text as shell code.\r\n\r\nPreserve unrelated files. If updating an untracked example would require inspecting it, leave it untouched and return a proposed draft ins"]
[0.000, "o", "tead. Complete independent sections when a requirement is unknown; ask only when the gap prevents a correct result.\r\n\r\nReport changes, checks, and unresolved inputs briefly. Distinguish parsing, application-schema validation, and provider verification. Commit, push, installation, publication, and provider mutations require separate authorization.\r\n\r\nFor the current annotation style, read the [annotated example](evals/annotated-example.md). For the earlier drafting evaluation, read the [synthetic contract](evals/contract.md). Its example is usable as an input only after it satisfies the tracked-example rule; it is not a universal starter.\r\n# Static Env-Spec Checker\r\n\r\nThis dependency-free checker validates a deliberately bounded subset of the [Env-Spec reference](https://varlock.dev/env-spec/reference/). It uses Node's `util.parseEnv` after a lexical gate. Run with Node 22 or newer. It never executes annotations, resolves configuration functions, loads an environment automatically, or validates the process env"]
[0.000, "o", "ironment.\r\n\r\n## API\r\n\r\n```js\r\nimport { checkEnvText } from './check-env-example.mjs';\r\nconst report = checkEnvText(newlyAuthoredText, { mode: 'template' });\r\n```\r\n\r\nReports contain `ok`, `complete`, `mode`, `count`, `missingRequired` (key names), and `diagnostics` (line, key, code, message). Reports omit values, source excerpts, documentation URLs, enum members, and raw exceptions. Key names are intentionally visible. Invalid/unsupported input sets `ok=false`. A required blank is valid in template mode but sets `complete=false`. Values mode rejects required blanks. Optional blanks skip type checks.\r\n\r\n## CLI\r\n\r\nRun these commands from the skill's `scripts/` directory. File mode also requires Git. Paths supplied to `--file` are relative to the current working directory.\r\n\r\n```sh\r\nnode check-env-example.mjs --stdin --mode template\r\nnode check-env-example.mjs --file path/to/.env.example --mode template\r\nnode --test check-env-example.test.mjs\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse stdin only for newly authored text. The CLI cannot establis"]
[0.000, "o", "h the provenance of piped input. Do not pipe existing private env files into it.\r\n\r\nFile mode accepts only the exact basename `.env.example`. It checks Git tracking and requires a regular blob committed at HEAD (mode 100644 or 100755). It reads that committed blob by object ID, never the working-tree env file. Consequently, uncommitted edits do not affect the check. A committed symlink is refused; a working-tree symlink replacing a committed regular file cannot redirect the blob read. The containing directory must exist and identify a Git repository. Input is capped at 1 MiB.\r\n\r\nExit statuses are 0 for a supported valid result, 1 for invalid/unsupported content, and 2 for command or input refusal. Importing the module does not run the CLI.\r\n\r\n## Supported Syntax\r\n\r\nPreceding comment blocks support actual `@required`, `@optional`, `@sensitive`, and `@public` decorators, either bare or with `=true`/`=false`. Required/optional and sensitive/public pairs cannot be combined, even when one is false. Root `@defaultR"]
[0.000, "o", "equired` and `@defaultSensitive` accept explicit booleans in the header. Their defaults are both true. Every assignment defaults to string type and needs no annotation.\r\n\r\nTypes are `@type=string`, `@type=boolean`, `@type=enum(debug, info)`, `@type=number(min=1, max=10, isInt=true)`, and `@type=port(min=1, max=65535)`. Number bounds must be finite decimals. Integer numbers must fit JavaScript's safe integer range. Ports always stay within 0 through 65535. Boolean values use the exact spellings `t,true,yes,on,1,f,false,no,off,0`. Enum members may be simple tokens or quoted static strings, including quoted commas or brackets.\r\n\r\nRepeated `@docs(https://docs.example.org/)` or `@docs(\"Label\", https://docs.example.org/)` annotations are accepted. URLs must use HTTP or HTTPS and contain no username/password. No URL is fetched. `@example=\"clearly synthetic\"` is accepted as metadata. Ordinary prose comments may interleave; an at-sign within prose is ignored. Blank lines and divider comments starting with `---` or `=="]
[0.000, "o", "=` break attachment. Detached item annotations produce errors.\r\n\r\nAssignments support a single line with plain, single-quoted, or double-quoted values. Plain inline comments and quoted hash characters are supported. Escapes, multiline values, interpolation, backticks, function expressions, inline decorators, unquoted arrays/objects, and unquoted `undefined`/`null` are refused. Quoted bracket characters remain literal strings. Unsupported decorators, types, and options fail closed. This is a static subset checker, not a full Varlock evaluator. Plain annotations do not enforce types in an ordinary dotenv loader. Cross-variable conditions are not checked. A variable omitted entirely cannot be detected without a separate inventory of the application's configuration consumers.\r\n\r\n## Verification Scope\r\n\r\nTests use only newly authored synthetic strings and a temporary synthetic Git repository. They cover type failures, required/optional behavior, roots, metadata attachment, duplicates, unsupported syntax, sanitized"]
[0.000, "o", " output, command errors, and committed blob isolation. The checker was tested with Node v26.6.0; compatibility with older supported Node versions was not separately executed.\r\n\r\n## Sources\r\n\r\nSupported annotation spelling and semantics were checked on 2026-10-09 against the [syntax reference](https://varlock.dev/env-spec/reference/), [item decorators](https://varlock.dev/reference/item-decorators/), [types](https://varlock.dev/reference/data-types/), and [root defaults](https://varlock.dev/reference/root-decorators/). This is a local static checker, not an upstream conformance implementation.\r\n\r\n## Layout Check and Formatter\r\n\r\nRun from the skill's `scripts/` directory with newly authored text on stdin:\r\n\r\n```sh\r\nnode format-env-example.mjs --check --stdin\r\nnode format-env-example.mjs --format --stdin\r\nnode --test format-env-example.test.mjs\r\n```\r\n\r\nCheck mode exits 0 for clean spacing, 1 for missing block spacing, and 2 for unsupported input. Diagnostics contain line numbers and fixed messages, never source "]
[0.000, "o", "text. Format mode prints the revised candidate; it has no file-reading or file-writing mode. Never pipe an actual runtime env file into it.\r\n\r\nThe rule separates a new prose-comment block from an immediately preceding active or commented assignment when another assignment follows the block. Annotation-only groups stay compact. Existing whitespace and nonblank text remain unchanged; formatting is idempotent. Shared group design and trailing-note placement remain editorial decisions. Multiline, escaped, and dynamic assignment syntax is refused conservatively.\r\n\r\nDashboard review is separate: check that a link targets the right credential or setting, not merely the provider's home page. Public documentation can establish the route pattern; it does not prove an authenticated page was opened. The formatter does not invent or verify URLs.\r\n# Annotated Example\r\n\r\nThis newly authored synthetic example demonstrates the supported static annotation subset. It is not an existing env file or a working GitHub integration. "]
[0.000, "o", "The hypothetical process requires a token; its API operations and permission scopes remain unspecified. Keep the token blank.\r\n\r\n```dotenv\r\n# SYNTHETIC EXAMPLE. Node.js dotenv values with @env-spec comments.\r\n\r\n# Inspect listeners: lsof -nP -iTCP:3000 -sTCP:LISTEN\r\n# @type=port(min=1)\r\nPORT=3000\r\n\r\n# @type=enum(debug, info, warn, error)\r\nLOG_LEVEL=info\r\n\r\n# Create or inspect: https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens\r\n# @docs(https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens#creating-a-fine-grained-personal-access-token)\r\n# @required @sensitive\r\nGITHUB_TOKEN=\r\n```\r\n\r\nExpected template result: three declarations, valid supported syntax and defaults, `complete: false`, and `GITHUB_TOKEN` listed as missing. In values mode, the blank required token fails. Neither mode verifies provider access or application behavior.\r\n\r\nThe prior five-variable fixture remains historical. This is a separate three-variable annotation fixture. Existing untrac"]
[0.000, "o", "ked env files are not read or overwritten.\r\n# Synthetic Environment Template Test\r\n\r\nThis is a deliberately synthetic configuration contract, created for testing the drafting skill. It is not a production application, and no provider connection is implemented. Values below are test inputs chosen for this fixture, not measurements or personal configuration.\r\n\r\n## Input Contract\r\n\r\nThe hypothetical process loads an env file once at startup using Node.js dotenv syntax. Use Node.js 22 or newer for the parsing check. All values remain strings until the process validates them.\r\n\r\n| Variable | Contract |\r\n| --- | --- |\r\n| PORT | Optional integer from 1 through 65535. Missing defaults to 3000. Empty is invalid. |\r\n| REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS | Optional positive integer in milliseconds. Missing defaults to 5000. Empty is invalid. |\r\n| ENABLE_GITHUB | Optional literal `true` or `false`. Missing defaults to `false`. Empty is invalid. |\r\n| GITHUB_TOKEN | Required and nonempty only when ENABLE_GITHUB is `true`. Server-only. Unus"]
[0.000, "o", "ed when disabled. |\r\n| BANNER | Optional display text. Missing defaults to `Local # demo`. Empty intentionally hides the banner. |\r\n\r\nThere are no browser variables or build-time settings. Changes take effect on the next process start. The hypothetical GitHub integration has no defined API operation, so its required permissions remain unknown. Do not guess scopes or claim provider readiness. The example should leave it disabled and explain the unresolved permission requirement.\r\n\r\nThe target example is `.env.example`; a developer's local copy would be `.env.local`. Do not overwrite an existing copy. No local copy or real token is needed for this test.\r\n\r\nUse these public references for acquisition and parser behavior:\r\n\r\n- [GitHub token creation](https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new). Requires login; authenticated behavior is not tested.\r\n- [GitHub token documentation](https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens).\r\n- [No"]
[0.000, "o", "de.js dotenv grammar](https://nodejs.org/api/environment_variables.html).\r\n- [Node.js parseEnv](https://nodejs.org/api/util.html#utilparseenvcontent).\r\n\r\n## Drafting Evaluation\r\n\r\nGive an independent agent this contract and the skill, without the authored example. Ask it to return newly authored template text in a Markdown artifact and validate the candidate string in memory. Do not read untracked env outputs. Do not grant access to live env files or allow provider calls.\r\n\r\nPass conditions:\r\n\r\n- It preserves all five variables and the documented missing/empty distinction.\r\n- The default example parses to PORT `3000`, REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS `5000`, ENABLE_GITHUB `false`, an empty GITHUB_TOKEN, and BANNER `Local # demo`.\r\n- It includes relevant docs and dashboard links and restart guidance. Add variable-specific one-liners only for useful operations supported by the input; no command quota.\r\n- It explains conditional requiredness and unknown permissions without inventing scopes.\r\n- It clearly labels the fixture sy"]
